## What I did

/def and /refs now forgive code-shaped queries — you can paste a token straight out of your editor and it still resolves the symbol.

The problem: `parse_def_query/parse_refs_query only trimmed whitespace, so /def foo()` searched for the literal symbol foo() (no match), /refs &mut buf searched for &mut buf, /def self.count searched for `self.count`. You'd get a misleading "no definition found for 'foo()'" when the definition was right there. Every language hits this.

**The fix** — one small pure helper, normalize_symbol_query(raw: &str) -> String, wired into both parse functions at the query-parse boundary (I did NOT touch the matching logic, so the fuzzy/substring paths keep working and now just receive a clean identifier):
• Takes the last maximal run of [A-Za-z0-9_]: foo() → foo, &mut foo → foo, self.count → count, `crate::bar::baz → baz, foo, → foo`.
• The < rule: if there's a <, only look at the text before it, so `Vec<Foo> → Vec and foo<T> → foo (instead of picking up the T`).
• No ident chars → returns the trimmed original (() stays ()), so weird input is graceful.
• **Char-safe** — pushes chars into a String per run, no byte indexing (café() → caf, no panic).

Discipline: tests first, both sides of each boundary (Day 122) — plain identifier unchanged, plus each code-shape hit, plus empty-stays-empty, no-ident fallback, and the multi-byte non-panic case. Also added `parse_def_query("/def foo()") == "foo" and the /refs` equivalent end-to-end.

Docs: added a one-line note to the /def entry in docs/src/usage/commands.md.

Verified: cargo fmt --check, `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings, cargo build, cargo test` all green.

A small forgiveness that makes the gesture feel like a real editor's "go to definition" instead of a brittle exact-name lookup.
